WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.968
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.836
is an Oscar and Emmy
Award-winning actress

00:00:03.836 --> 00:00:05.604
you know from films such as
"Marriage Story"

00:00:05.604 --> 00:00:07.206
and "Jurassic Park"
and shows like

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:08.575
"Big Little Lies"
and "Enlightened."

00:00:08.575 --> 00:00:11.143
She is also the coauthor
of the new book

00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:13.746 line:0%
"Honey, Baby, Mine:
A Mother and Daughter Talk

00:00:13.746 --> 00:00:16.015 line:0%
Life, Death,
Love and Banana Pudding,"

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:17.583 line:0%
which is now available
everywhere.

00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:20.219
Please welcome back to the show
Laura Dern, everybody.

00:00:20.219 --> 00:00:21.487
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:21.487 --> 00:00:36.702 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:36.702 --> 00:00:39.405
Welcome back.
-Hi, everybody.

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:42.776
[ Cheers and applause continue ]

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:45.345
-I always genuinely love
talking to you.

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:47.046
And what I love
about this book

00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:49.649
is I feel like I'm talking
with you when you read it

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:51.851
because this is a conversation
between you and your mother.

00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:54.120
And this is the kind
of conversation, I guess,

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.923
that you guys had never
had together before.

00:00:56.923 --> 00:00:59.726
What was the sort of catalyst
to approaching

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:01.360
having these conversations
together?

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.565
-So my amazing mother,
Diane Ladd,

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:09.735
who is such an extraordinary
actor and been,

00:01:09.735 --> 00:01:14.440
you know, the light of my life,
got a health diagnosis.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.910
She unknowingly --
In her neighborhood

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:21.047
were surrounding farms
and they were using pesticides

00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:23.515
and spraying
without notification.

00:01:23.515 --> 00:01:27.820
And when she discovered
there was this lung disease

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.356
from scarring
from the pesticide exposure,

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:34.593
they said "your mom likely
has 3 to 6 months to live"

00:01:34.593 --> 00:01:37.697
and there was no medicine
or support,

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:42.802
so they said, so they hoped
that by walking

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:45.905
she might get more strength
and expand her lungs.

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:48.407
And so she really
didn't want to do this.

00:01:48.407 --> 00:01:50.376
Let's be clear, she said,
"I hate walking.

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:53.078
I had to walk 10 miles
to school when I was little.

00:01:53.078 --> 00:01:54.980
Don't make me walk more."

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.382
And at the time
she was on oxygen.

00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:59.318
It was very difficult,
and she loves telling stories.

00:01:59.318 --> 00:02:01.787
So I said, "I'll tell you what,
we're going to walk each day

00:02:01.787 --> 00:02:03.856
a little bit further
and we're going to talk

00:02:03.856 --> 00:02:05.859
and I'm going to record it
in my phone,

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:07.493
and your grandchildren
will have it."

00:02:07.493 --> 00:02:09.462
And as my mother says,
you know,

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:11.096
when you think
you're going to go,

00:02:11.096 --> 00:02:13.566
you tell each other everything.

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:17.103
But the gift of the experience
was we discovered,

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:18.537
as close as we are,

00:02:18.537 --> 00:02:21.074
and we're actors who've
worked together on films --

00:02:21.074 --> 00:02:22.908
she was also
in "Enlightened" with me

00:02:22.908 --> 00:02:26.412
and David Lynch's "Wild at
Heart" and "Rambling Rose" --

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:28.847
we had spoken about so little.

00:02:28.847 --> 00:02:31.917
We had skirted from the deepest

00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:33.920
and most heartbreaking
of moments in our life,

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.455
and I never asked
her things like,

00:02:36.455 --> 00:02:37.657
"What's the first movie
you ever saw?

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:39.892
Why did you want
to become an actor?"

00:02:39.892 --> 00:02:43.830
And this was the discovery
of an intimacy

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.699
we don't share enough
with our closest of loved ones.

00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:50.403
-And then the real happy
ending as well is health-wise.

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:51.504
She's okay?

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:53.373
-Also, to quote my mom,

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:55.941
this occurred
at the beginning of 2019,

00:02:55.941 --> 00:02:58.577
and she will tell you,
"Four years later,

00:02:58.577 --> 00:03:01.180
I've done two movies,
a TV show and wrote a book."

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:02.448
So...

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:06.919
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:06.919 --> 00:03:11.524
But she does believe
that the walking did in fact

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:15.294
give her more energy and that
the talking and the healing

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:19.332
and us loving each other
was a major healing

00:03:19.332 --> 00:03:22.568
and that she didn't
believe doctors.

00:03:22.568 --> 00:03:25.304
She was like, "They're
'practicing' medicine, Laura."

00:03:25.304 --> 00:03:27.206
[ Laughter ]

00:03:27.206 --> 00:03:29.308
So she's like, "I'm going to get
several other opinions."

00:03:29.308 --> 00:03:31.544
And she believes
in alternative options

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:33.278
and worked very hard
to be here.

00:03:33.278 --> 00:03:35.348
-Well, I'm so happy she's here.

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:36.816
One thing that must
have been very surreal

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:38.785
is, so, you record her
on these walks

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:40.986
and then obviously
you transcribe it into a book

00:03:40.986 --> 00:03:43.021
and then -- which is
a great idea, obviously,

00:03:43.021 --> 00:03:45.124
because you have
a couple of actors

00:03:45.124 --> 00:03:47.360
who have written a book,
you record the audiobook,

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:48.994
so now you're back
in the booth,

00:03:48.994 --> 00:03:52.998
basically rerecording a
conversation you've already had.

00:03:52.998 --> 00:03:54.433
Were there ever times
where you thought,

00:03:54.433 --> 00:03:56.535
"Oh, now that I'm getting
another chance at this story,

00:03:56.535 --> 00:03:57.870
I'd love to..."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:00.172
-Yes. I mean,
you had two actresses,

00:04:00.172 --> 00:04:03.175
so it felt like doing
a one-act play or something,

00:04:03.175 --> 00:04:05.278
but it was so personal.

00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:08.014
And in the moment
we were sharing everything.

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:10.116
And the only reason
we ended up doing this

00:04:10.116 --> 00:04:12.719
was, as we started
sharing our story,

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:14.153
it was inspiring

00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:17.756
friends, my children, people,
all different relationships

00:04:17.756 --> 00:04:21.393
to communicate more empathically
and knowing

00:04:21.393 --> 00:04:24.397
that you don't necessarily
solve anything or agree,

00:04:24.397 --> 00:04:26.999
but you ask the questions
and you hear each other.

00:04:26.999 --> 00:04:30.402
But once we were in there and
we heard some of the responses,

00:04:30.402 --> 00:04:33.806
there is a story about my mother
taking my son for a haircut

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:37.043
when I was out of town,
and I'm still livid.

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:40.079
And we did the audio recording
and at the end of it I was like,

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:42.782
"You are so wrong.
I can't believe you did that."

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:45.217
And we got in a huge fight.
We needed to take five, guys.

00:04:45.217 --> 00:04:47.386
-It must be interesting
because the first time

00:04:47.386 --> 00:04:48.821
you're listening,
you're thinking,

00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:50.589
"Well, she's only going to be
around for another three months"

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:52.024
and now she's fine.

00:04:52.024 --> 00:04:54.793
You're like, "I got a -- I have
a different answer."

00:04:54.793 --> 00:04:57.029
[ Laughter ]
-That's what she says.

00:04:57.029 --> 00:04:58.598
She was like, "Wait a minute.

00:04:58.598 --> 00:05:02.201
Why did I go through
that whole story with her?"

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:04.037
-You know, one of the things --
You guys, your first film

00:05:04.037 --> 00:05:08.907
was a film your mother was in,
and Martin Scorsese directed it.

00:05:08.907 --> 00:05:10.910
And an interesting thing
is your mom talks

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:15.715
about how she had anxiety
when you told her

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:19.352
that you wanted to share the
same profession that she had.

00:05:19.352 --> 00:05:20.753
And it's funny
because I had nothing,

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:22.155
you know,
I shouldn't say nothing.

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:23.690
I had a lot of anxiety
in my career.

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:25.324
I've had so much more joy
than anxiety.

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:26.893
But if my kids said
they wanted to do it,

00:05:26.893 --> 00:05:28.361
I would have the same thing.

00:05:28.361 --> 00:05:30.763
Weirdly, I would think about the
downside more than the upside.

00:05:30.763 --> 00:05:32.231
-Yeah.
-Was that the first time

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:34.433
she had shared with you that she
was worried when you said

00:05:34.433 --> 00:05:35.634
you wanted this path?
-I mean,

00:05:35.634 --> 00:05:38.604
at the very beginning
she was very reticent.

00:05:38.604 --> 00:05:41.440
But I was a kid.
I mean, I did the, you know,

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:43.075
played an extra
in my mom's movie,

00:05:43.075 --> 00:05:44.777
"Alice Doesn't Live Here
Anymore" with Scorsese.

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:46.779
And I was a 7-year-old.

00:05:46.779 --> 00:05:52.151
But I fell in love with this,
for me, incredible team sport.

00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:55.287
It was the first time I had sort
of seen a team sport at work.

00:05:55.287 --> 00:05:58.891
150 people come together
to make one thing,

00:05:58.891 --> 00:06:01.861
as you all well know,
on this incredible show.

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:04.764
You know, it feels so good
when it works, right?

00:06:04.764 --> 00:06:07.866
It doesn't always. But anyway.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:07.866 --> 00:06:09.468
But this always works.

00:06:09.468 --> 00:06:11.136
-Of course.
-So anyway, just so we're clear.

00:06:11.136 --> 00:06:12.404
-We keep thinking we're
going to have a night

00:06:12.404 --> 00:06:14.040
where something falls flat.
But yeah, not yet.

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:17.176
-No, no, but it's, you know,

00:06:17.176 --> 00:06:20.146
it's so invigorating
and she saw my passion.

00:06:20.146 --> 00:06:22.548
But as you said,
she also saw the pitfalls,

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:25.785
especially as a young girl
of entering an industry

00:06:25.785 --> 00:06:29.221
where she couldn't always
be protective of me.

00:06:29.221 --> 00:06:32.024
So she tried to talk me
out of it, but it didn't work.

00:06:32.024 --> 00:06:35.361
-And there's a lovely story
about, as an 11-year-old,

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:37.763
you took steps to further
your own career at a party.

00:06:37.763 --> 00:06:40.166
-[ Laughs ]
Yes, it's so ridiculous.

00:06:40.166 --> 00:06:42.969
But it is true that I was 11
at my mom's friend,

00:06:42.969 --> 00:06:44.670
the actor Bo Hopkins' house.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:47.206
And I went up to an agent
and I said,

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:49.175
"My mom won't help me
and I want to be an actor.

00:06:49.175 --> 00:06:50.910
Will you let me audition
for you?"

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:52.978
[ Laughter ]

00:06:52.978 --> 00:06:55.047
-It's the most Hollywood thing

00:06:55.047 --> 00:06:56.381
to bring your kid
to a party and be like,

00:06:56.381 --> 00:06:57.983
"Now, don't -- If I catch you
talking to agents..."

00:06:57.983 --> 00:07:00.619
-Yeah, stay away from the
agents.

00:07:00.619 --> 00:07:02.254
-I have a lot more questions.

00:07:02.254 --> 00:07:04.357
We will be right back with more
from Laura Dern.

